    {
      "kind": "statement",
      "eyebrow": "01 · The thesis",
      "title": "The technology is not the story. The cost line is.",
      "body": "Energy is a capital allocation business with commodity price exposure. Technology improves recovery, reduces downtime and shortens the learning curve on new capacity, but it rarely escapes the price cycle. The new demand source is data centres and electrification, which pulls gas, grid and nuclear into the same investment conversation."
    },
    {
      "kind": "points",
      "eyebrow": "02 · Sector economics",
      "title": "What this sector actually sells",
      "body": "Revenue is price times volume; cost is lifting cost, royalties and capex. Reserve life and decline rates determine terminal value, and refining or LNG spreads add a second, cyclical margin.",
      "bullets": [
        "Upstream economics depend on lifting cost and decline rates, not headline production.",
        "Integrated majors smooth the cycle with refining, chemicals and trading.",
        "LNG earns on contracted spreads and liquefaction utilisation.",
        "Oilfield services are cyclical, with utilisation and pricing power in tight markets.",
        "Cost of capital and reserve life drive valuation more than near-term prices."
      ]
    },
    {
